#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Assemble publication documents from fixed inputs and numeric rehearsal records."""
import csv,hashlib,pathlib,re,subprocess
C=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
def digest(p):return hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
def table(paths):
 return '| File | SHA256 |\n|---|---|\n'+''.join(f'| `{p}` | `{digest(C/p)}` |\n' for p in paths)
def release_table(systems):
 rows=list(csv.DictReader((C/'releases.tsv').open(),delimiter='\t'))
 return '| System | Release | Image checksum |\n|---|---|---|\n'+''.join(f'| {r["system"]} | {r["release"]} | {r["checksum_algorithm"]} `{r["published_checksum"]}` |\n' for r in rows if r['system'] in systems and not r['reuse'].startswith('superseded'))
def recipes(systems):
 return [p for p in (C/'rehearsal/recipe-paths.txt').read_text().splitlines() if any(p.startswith('install/recipes/'+s+'/') for s in systems)]
intro='Prepared 2026-11-23 UTC. These files are concrete pre-observation inputs. No classification or property assessment has been made. They must be committed before the first observation; this preparation does not assert that a repository commit exists. Readiness is an instrument result, not a hypothesis outcome.\n\n'
shared=['install/materialize.py','lab.sh','collect.sh','collector/main.py','collector/packages.py','collector/portage-reasons.py','collector/rpm-selected-groups.py','collector/talos.py','collector/probe.go','collector/btrfs_linux.go','collector/build_probe.py']+sorted(p.relative_to(C).as_posix() for p in (C/'collector').glob('probe-linux-amd64.gz.part-*'))
keynote='Installation recipes are published as credential-free `.in` templates under `install/recipes/`. `install/materialize.py LAB_ROOT PUBLIC_KEY_FILE --previous-lab-root PREVIOUS_LAB_ROOT` reconstructs their relative paths, materializes explicit laboratory roots, and inserts the separately generated laboratory public key. The key material is excluded from publication; its digests are disclosed separately. Talos creates fresh credentials with its pinned release client; generated client/machine configurations are excluded. Historical failed recipes are retained and labelled by their README and CHOICES; they are not substitutes for active successful recipes.\n\n'
systems=['alma','void','gentoo','microos','talos']
ordinary=['actions/alma.sh','actions/void.sh','actions/gentoo.sh','actions/ordinary.sh','actions/lib.sh','actions/firefox-addon.py','actions/gentoo-firefox-addon.py','actions/ordinary.md','actions/README.md']
s='# Check 1 concrete inputs\n\n'+intro+release_table(systems)+'\nRelease URLs, published checksum references, cutoff and verification provenance are fixed in `releases.tsv`. The cutoff is 2026-11-23T01:19:00Z. The existing installer-choice record is fixed in `CHOICES.md`; review finding 8 identifies missing image-variant rationales, disclosed in `FIX-C1-REH-1.md` without changing that record. No new workload choice is inferred from guest contents.\n\n'
s+='Availability and rehearsal acceptance are reported in `READY.md`. All five systems remain the prescribed case list. A system that is not ready has not been silently removed from the check. Late-system preparation uses the final KIT2 inputs when available; readiness is stated explicitly in the accompanying table.\n\n'+keynote
s+='## Installation recipes\n\n'+table(recipes(systems)+['install/materialize.py'])
s+='\n## Ordinary actions and exact commands\n\nOnly AlmaLinux, Void and Gentoo receive ordinary actions. Their nine activities remain the exact Section 9.2 categories. The system entry scripts below specify the selected programs and commands, including disclosed same-category replacements needed for unattended execution. `DISCLOSURES.md` records each repair and replacement. Instrument synchronization changes retain the same activity; every change is disclosed.\n\nStage the action directory in a fresh clone and invoke as the ordinary account `user`: `bash alma.sh`, `bash void.sh`, or `bash gentoo.sh`. The launcher in `rehearsal/fx-run.py` supplies a detached session so network changes cannot terminate the batch. These are the exact script sources and hashes, including shared helpers:\n\n'+table(ordinary)
for p in ordinary[:6]:
 s+='\n### '+p+'\n\n```'+('python' if p.endswith('.py') else 'bash')+'\n'+(C/p).read_text()+'```\n'
s+='\n## Collector and recorded facts\n\nThe observation command is `LAB_ROOT=LAB_ROOT C1_INPUTS_COMMIT=COMMIT C1/collect.sh --observe MACHINE OUTPUT`. Replace the two uppercase path/commit values and the synthetic machine/output names with the committed check invocation. Rehearsals use the same queries and normalization through `--rehearsal`, with a fresh `reh-` overlay and automatic numerical reduction/deletion. They differ in query-label anonymization and retention, not query selection.\n\n'+table(shared)+ '\n`COLLECT.md`, `collector/PACKAGES.md` and `TALOS-COLLECT.md` define exact query sources, limits and unavailable facts. Level 1 records packages, services and timers, processes/executables, accounts/groups, access rules, module/driver names, sockets/firewall state, network records and container/image identities. Level 2 records stored objects with unresolved or absent ownership and a separate executable-code list. Group members retain type, mode, owner, size, UTC mtime, executable bit, bounded signature and persistence. Package facts include explicit/dependency/unknown reason and file integrity against native installation digests. Configuration-origin timestamps do not prove creation time; unresolved provenance remains unknown. Missing tools or inaccessible APIs are not evidence of absence.\n\n'
s+='## Classification and adjudication: five lines from Section 9.2\n\n```text\nEach object is decided twice, independently: an installed object, a program, a setting, a program record,\nmaterial or what exists only while the computer runs; whether the object must be recorded and by which line.\nOn any machine, an object is found for which the text of Sections 8.1 and 8.2 does not allow deciding\nwhat it is and whether it must be recorded, or allows deciding both ways; or the two classifications decided\ndifferently and the adjudication finds no sentence of the text that resolves the dispute.\n```\n\nThe last three lines are the failure condition. Section 9.1 incorporates Appendix A.4.1: classifications are separate and cannot see each other; adjudication is made by neither classification and quotes the resolving sentence. No agreement threshold or additional decision rule is added.\n\nA machine whose second-level inventory has not been obtained cannot yield 1: its outcome is 0 if an object meeting the Section 9.2 failure condition is found on it, and ? otherwise. Talos exposed-object rows do not establish a complete second-level inventory. The same rule applies to any incomplete generic traversal.\n'
(C/'inputs-check-1.md').write_text(s)
s='# Check 4 concrete inputs\n\n'+intro+release_table(['nixos','guix'])+'\nRelease selection and checksum references are fixed in `releases.tsv`. '+keynote
s+='NixOS uses the preserved declaration plus the successful instrument-access addition. Guix uses its unchanged original declaration and original serial access; failed SSH reconfigurations are historical inputs, not applied to its active base. `READY.md` records the twenty-call transport rehearsals and the Guix shutdown fallback. No collector or property probe runs on these two systems during rehearsal.\n\n## Installation recipes\n\n'+table(recipes(['nixos','guix'])+['install/materialize.py','lab.sh'])
s+='\n## Facts for the three properties\n\nAppendix A.4.5 names exactly these facts, applied to the three properties of Section 8.3:\n\n- P1: whether objects that must be recorded can be in force without being recorded.\n- P2: whether recorded lines are in force after application.\n- P4: whether a built-in tool can change, past the declaration, what must be recorded.\n\nThe cases are NixOS/P1, NixOS/P2, NixOS/P4, Guix/P1, Guix/P2 and Guix/P4. Each is assessed twice independently from documentation and a clean installation, with a source or experimental record. A finding that a system lacks a property is a successful assessment, not check failure. The sufficiency rule, adjudication and outcomes are exactly Appendix A.4.5; no property has been assessed here.\n'
(C/'inputs-check-4.md').write_text(s)
lived=[('fedora','developer','lived-developer'),('mint','gaming','lived-gaming'),('debian-server','server','lived-server')]
s='# Lived-in inputs outside the plan\n\n'+intro+'These three disposable machines are outside the outcomes of the four checks and are reported separately, exactly as PROTOCOL.md Section 5 requires. Their actions are fixed before any first retained inventory. Rehearsal counts are not hypothesis outcomes.\n\n| Machine | Base | Entry command |\n|---|---|---|\n| Developer | Preserved clean Fedora Workstation installation with lab-access overlay | `bash lived-developer.sh` |\n| Gaming | Preserved clean Linux Mint Cinnamon installation with lab-access overlay | `bash lived-gaming.sh` |\n| Server | Debian 13 Trixie generic amd64 cloud image, build 20261116-2664; 8 GiB / 4 vCPU / 40 GiB | `bash lived-server.sh` |\n\n'+keynote
for sys,kind,stem in lived:
 s+='## '+kind.title()+' actions\n\n'+(C/f'actions/{stem}.md').read_text()+'\n'+table([f'actions/{stem}.sh',f'actions/{stem}.md','actions/lib.sh']+(['actions/gui_guard.py','actions/apt_https_sources.py'] if sys=='mint' else []))+'\n'
s+='## Base recipes\n\n'+table(recipes(['fedora','mint','debian'])+['install/materialize.py','lab.sh'])
s+='\nThe collector and recorded facts are the same committed files specified in `inputs-check-1.md`. `READY.md` and `DISCLOSURES.md` report incomplete actions and collection honestly; readiness is not asserted merely because a command returned zero.\n'
(C/'inputs-beyond-plan.md').write_text(s)
